    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-67104",
      "knowledge_point_id": "bd32a853-e0ec-5d56-bd6b-1c2e0e3249f8",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Formula for the Sum of a Geometric Series Given an Arbitrary Term and the Common Ratio",
      "problem": "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $4$th term is $192$ and the common ratio is $4?$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine ${a}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\ 192 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{(4-1)} \\\\ 192 & =64{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=3$ and $r=4.$ Substituting our values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{3(1-{4}^{N})}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-3)}(1-{4}^{N}) \\\\  & =-(1-{4}^{N}) \\\\  & ={4}^{N}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
